{"entries":{"chapter-42-study-frailty|Chapter 42 Study: Frailty":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Frailty (consensus definition): medical syndrome, multiple causes, ↓ strength/endurance and ↓ physiologic function → ↑ vulnerability to dependency/death; should not overlap with disability, and not exhaustively defined by sarcopenia or multimorbidity; screening endorsed for all individuals 70 and older (p617)":["u3"],"copy|Physical Frailty Phenotype (Fried/Cardiovascular Health Study) — 5 criteria: unintentional weight loss, weakness, exhaustion, slowness, low activity (p619) ≥3 of 5 criteria = frail; 2 of 5 = prefrail; 1 alone = risk factor only, not frailty itself (p619) — note Table 42-3 instead scores intermediate/prefrail as 1–2 criteria (p621) Weight loss: >10 lb lost unintentionally in the prior year (p620) Weakness: grip strength lowest 20% (stratified by gender/BMI) (p620) Slowness: 15-ft walk time, slowest 20% (by gender/height) (p620) Low activity: lowest 20% kcal/wk; men cutoff <383 kcal/wk (p620) Drawbacks: no cognitive dimension; weight-loss criterion can miss sarcopenic-obesity frailty; thresholds derived from CHS cohort (p620)":["u4"],"copy|Deficit Accumulation / Frailty Index (FI) (Rockwood): unweighted ratio of deficits present ÷ total considered, built from a series of 70 measurements (\"deficits\") (p620, p623); rises nonlinearly with age (Gompertz-like), higher in women, ceiling FI ~0.7 (p623)":["u5"],"copy|Clinical Frailty Scale (CFS): the original seven-category version used clinical judgment and predicted institutionalization and death similarly to the FI (p623). The current CFS has nine levels.":["u6"],"copy|Edmonton Frail Scale (EFS): 9 domains — cognition, general health, function, social support, meds, nutrition, mood, continence, performance (p623)":["u7"],"copy|Cognitive frailty: physical frailty + mild cognitive impairment (CDR 0.5), with AD/dementia excluded; it has been suggested that physical frailty should precede the cognitive impairment, to separate physically driven cognitive decline (p627)":["u8"],"copy|Table 42-3 (p621) catalogs further named frailty instruments beyond FPP/FI/CFS/EFS — Gill Frailty Measure, Brief Frailty Instrument, Vulnerable Elders Survey (VES-13), FRAIL Scale (p621–622) Table 42-3 also spells out the original CFS’s seven categories (p621): 1 = very fit; 2 = well (no active disease); 3 = well, with treated comorbid disease; 4 = apparently vulnerable (not frankly dependent, \"slowed up\"); 5 = mildly frail (IADL help only); 6 = moderately frail (help with IADLs + ADLs); 7 = severely frail (ADL-dependent) or terminally ill. The current nine-point CFS scores terminal illness as 9. VES-13: frail if score ≥3 (range 0–10); FRAIL Scale (fatigue, resistance, ambulation, ≥5 of 11 specified illnesses, weight loss ≥5% over the preceding year): 0 = no frailty, 1–2 = intermediate, ≥3 = frailty (p622; validated FRAIL questionnaire, Morley et al., PMCID PMC4515112).":["u9"],"copy|Community-dwelling age ≥65: average prevalence 11% (reported range 4.0–59% across definitions/settings) (p626)":["u10"],"copy|Broader definitions > Fried tool: 14% vs 10% (p626)":["u11"],"copy|Rises with age: 16% at 80–84 → 26% at ≥85 (p626)":["u12"],"copy|Women > men: Fried 9.6% vs 5.2%; FI 39% vs 37% (p626)":["u13"],"copy|Hospitalized inpatients: 27–80%; institutionalized: 30–70% (p626)":["u14"],"copy|Exhaustion is typically the first CHS criterion to appear (p626)":["u15"],"copy|Nearly 60% of adults >70 transition between frailty states at least once over 4.5 years; progression to a worse state is more common than recovery, and frail→nonfrail reversal is rare (p626)":["u16"],"copy|Concentric layers: outer = clinical presentation (multimorbidity, physical/cognitive impairment); middle = biomarker layer (inflammation, hormones, body composition, energetics); inner = core biological hallmarks of aging (p617)":["u17"],"copy|\"Inflammaging\": chronic elevation of IL-6 and TNF-α, present even in disease-free older adults (p624); accumulating senescent cells spilling SASP molecules may account, at least in part, for the high circulating inflammatory markers (p625)":["u18"],"copy|Multi-hormonal decline: DHEAS, testosterone, estrogens, GH/IGF-1, vitamin D ↓, with relative preservation of cortisol/thyroid hormones (p625)":["u19"],"copy|Muscle energy demand rises as much as 100-fold from rest to contraction — mitochondrial decline + reduced satellite-cell repair capacity impair this reserve (p625)":["u20"],"copy|RMR = 60–70% of total daily energy expenditure; initially rises (compensation), later falls as frailty develops — hypothesized that this RMR shift marks failure of resilience and accelerated, irreversible deterioration (p626)":["u21"],"copy|Surgery: frailty predicts postop pneumonia, delirium, UTI, longer stay, nursing-home discharge, and higher mortality; a multidimensional frailty score was more useful than conventional methods for predicting surgical outcomes (p627)":["u22"],"copy|CKD: CHS participants with CKD had 2-fold risk of frailty + disability; frail patients start dialysis earlier (higher eGFR) with no proven survival/functional benefit (p628)":["u23"],"copy|CVD: frailty ~3× more prevalent with CVD; CAD in women → de novo frailty 12% vs 5% over 6 yr; frail elders develop CAD events at 3.6% vs 2.8%/yr (p628)":["u24"],"copy|HIV: frailty prevalence 5–20% in younger HIV+ patients; risk ↑ with CD4 <350 cells/mm³, further risk factors: CD4 <100 cells/mm³, clinical AIDS, previous opportunistic illnesses (p628)":["u25"],"copy|Transplant: MELD predicts waitlist mortality but predicts posttransplant outcomes only at scores above 35; Fried-criteria frailty tracked quality of life better than MELD severity in end-stage liver disease (p629)":["u26"],"recall|Recall":["u27"],"copy|CHAPTER 42s / HAZZARD":["u28"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 42 — Frailty; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u29"],"copy|Definitions & diagnostic models":["u30"],"copy|Epidemiology":["u31"],"copy|Pathophysiology (\"onion\" model, Fig 42-1)":["u32"],"copy|Frailty in specific disease contexts":["u33"],"copy|1.":["u34"],"copy|What are the 5 components of the Fried physical frailty phenotype?":["u35"],"copy|Unintentional weight loss, weakness, exhaustion, slowness, and low activity (p619).":["u36"],"copy|2.":["u37"],"copy|How many of the 5 Fried criteria define frailty, and how many define prefrailty?":["u38"],"copy|≥3 of 5 = frail; 2 of 5 = prefrail; 1 alone is a risk factor, not frailty (p619).":["u39"],"copy|(Table 42-3 scores intermediate/prefrail as 1–2 criteria, p621.)":["u40"],"copy|4.":["u41"],"copy|What distance is walked to test the \"slowness\" criterion, and what defines an abnormal result?":["u42"],"copy|15-ft walk time; abnormal = slowest 20%, stratified by gender and height (p620).":["u43"],"copy|10.":["u44"],"copy|What is the average prevalence of frailty in community-dwelling adults ≥65, and what is the reported range?":["u45"],"copy|Average 11%, reported range 4.0%–59% depending on definition/setting (p626).":["u46"],"copy|11.":["u47"],"copy|How does frailty prevalence differ between ages 80–84 and ≥85?":["u48"],"copy|16% at ages 80–84 vs 26% at ages 85 and older (p626).":["u49"],"copy|14.":["u50"],"copy|What CD4+ T-cell count thresholds are linked to HIV-related frailty risk?":["u51"],"copy|CD4 <350 cells/mm³ (a marker of advanced HIV disease) is independently associated with a frailty-related phenotype; CD4 <100 cells/mm³ is a further risk factor, as are clinical AIDS and previous opportunistic illnesses (p628).":["u52"],"copy|Answer":["u53","u54","u55","u56","u57","u58"],"copy|Table 42-3 (p621) catalogs further named frailty instruments beyond FPP/FI/CFS/EFS — Gill Frailty Measure, Brief Frailty Instrument, Vulnerable Elders Survey (VES-13), FRAIL Scale (p621–622) Table 42-3 also spells out the original CFS’s seven categories (p621): 1 = very fit; 2 = well (no active disease); 3 = well, with treated comorbid disease; 4 = apparently vulnerable (not frankly dependent, \"slowed up\"); 5 = mildly frail (IADL help only); 6 = moderately frail (help with IADLs + ADLs); 7 = severely frail (ADL-dependent) or terminally ill. The current nine-point CFS scores terminal illness as 9. VES-13: frail if score ≥3 (range 0–10). FRAIL Scale: fatigue, resistance, ambulation, more than 5 illnesses, and more than 5% weight loss. Score 0 = no frailty, 1–2 = intermediate, ≥3 = frailty (Table 42-3, p622).Current practice (not the book): the validation study’s Methods scored 5 or more of 11 illnesses and at least 5% weight loss over 12 months. Its appendix uses >5% for weight loss. Morley et al., 2012, Methods and appendix.":["u9"]},"maxId":203,"sequenceOffsets":[[204,-145],[349,0]]}
